"""PTE Listening Bank 3: Highlight Correct Summary (50 items)
Canonical type_id: 'pte-highlight-correct-summary'
Live type_name: 'Highlight Correct Summary'

Each item: (title, recording, correct summary, three wrong summaries, why).
Wrong summaries reverse the main point, misstate a key detail, or turn a side
point into the main one. The correct summary rotates through A-D.
"""

HCS_RAW = [
    ("Countdown timers at crossings",
     "Many cities have installed countdown timers at pedestrian crossings, showing how many seconds remain before the lights change. The idea is simple: if people know exactly how much time they have, they can decide whether it is safe to start crossing, and fewer will be caught in the middle of the road when traffic starts moving. Several studies have found that pedestrians generally like the timers and feel safer with them. However, the timers can also be seen by drivers. Some research suggests that drivers approaching a junction use the countdown to judge whether they can get through before the light turns red, and some of them speed up. For this reason, a few cities have experimented with timers that are visible only to pedestrians, or have combined them with longer crossing times. The overall effect on safety appears to depend on the design of the junction.",
     "Countdown timers help pedestrians judge whether they have time to cross, and many people feel safer with them. However, drivers can also see the timers and may speed up, so their effect on safety depends on how junctions are designed.",
     ["Countdown timers have been shown to eliminate accidents at junctions completely, because both drivers and pedestrians slow down as soon as they see the number of seconds remaining before the lights change.",
      "Most pedestrians dislike countdown timers because they create pressure to hurry, so many cities have removed them from busy junctions and replaced them with longer crossing times and traditional signals instead.",
      "Countdown timers were designed mainly for drivers to help them decide when to brake, and research shows that pedestrians rarely notice the numbers, so the timers have little effect on how people cross."],
     "Pedestrians benefit, but drivers may use the timers to speed up."),
    ("Rules on parking spaces",
     "For much of the twentieth century, many cities required every new building to include a minimum number of parking spaces. A block of flats might need at least one space per apartment, and a restaurant a certain number per table. These rules were intended to prevent a shortage of on-street parking, but critics argue that they have had costly side effects. Parking takes up valuable land and can add significantly to the cost of building homes, costs that are passed on to residents whether or not they own a car. By guaranteeing cheap or free parking everywhere, the rules may also encourage people to drive rather than walk or use public transport. In recent years, a growing number of cities have removed their minimum parking requirements. Supporters point out that this does not ban parking: developers can still build as many spaces as they think their customers want.",
     "Rules requiring new buildings to include parking were meant to protect street parking, but they raise building costs and may encourage driving. Many cities are now removing them, while still allowing developers to provide parking where there is demand.",
     ["Cities introduced parking requirements to reduce car use, and removing them has led to bans on building new car parks in many areas, forcing residents to rely completely on public transport.",
      "Minimum parking rules have lowered the cost of housing because developers share the price of spaces with local governments, so most cities are now making the requirements even stricter than before.",
      "Parking requirements mainly affect restaurants, which must provide one space for every table, and critics say this has forced many small businesses in city centres to close over recent decades."],
     "The rules raised costs and encouraged driving, and removing them does not ban parking."),
    ("Singapore's hawker centres",
     "In the 1960s and 1970s, the government of Singapore began moving thousands of street food sellers, known as hawkers, off the streets and into purpose-built food centres. The main reasons were hygiene and public health: food was being prepared without clean water or proper waste disposal. The new hawker centres provided running water, drainage and regular inspections, while allowing sellers to continue offering cheap, freshly cooked dishes. Over time, the centres became an important part of daily life, places where people of different backgrounds and incomes eat side by side. In 2020, Singapore's hawker culture was added to UNESCO's list of intangible cultural heritage. Yet its future faces challenges. Many hawkers are elderly, the work involves long hours for modest profits, and fewer young people are willing to take it on, so schemes have been introduced to train and support new hawkers.",
     "Singapore moved street food sellers into hawker centres to improve hygiene, and the centres became central to everyday life and were recognised by UNESCO. However, ageing sellers and hard working conditions threaten their future, so programmes now support new hawkers.",
     ["Singapore banned street food in the 1960s because it was too expensive for most residents, and the new hawker centres were built mainly for tourists, who now make up most of their customers.",
      "Hawker centres were created to preserve traditional recipes, and their recognition by UNESCO has attracted so many young people that there is now a long waiting list for anyone who wants to become a hawker.",
      "The main problem with Singapore's hawker centres today is poor hygiene, since they have no running water or drainage, and inspectors have recommended moving the food sellers back onto the streets."],
     "Hygiene drove the move, the centres became heritage, and ageing hawkers threaten their future."),
    ("Bus bunching",
     "Anyone who has waited a long time for a bus, only to see two or three arrive together, has experienced bus bunching. It happens even when buses leave the depot at perfectly regular intervals. Suppose one bus is delayed slightly, perhaps by traffic. By the time it reaches the next stop, more passengers have gathered, so boarding takes longer and the bus falls further behind. Meanwhile, the bus following it finds fewer passengers waiting, moves faster, and gradually catches up. Eventually the two travel together, leaving a long gap behind them. The problem is self-reinforcing, so small delays grow over the length of a route. Transport operators use several methods to reduce it. Drivers can be asked to wait at certain stops to restore even spacing, passengers can be allowed to board through all doors, and dedicated bus lanes reduce the delays that start the process.",
     "Buses bunch because a slightly delayed bus meets more waiting passengers and slows further, while the bus behind speeds up and catches it. Holding buses at stops, boarding through all doors and bus lanes can reduce the problem.",
     ["Buses arrive together mainly because depots send them out in groups to save money on drivers, and the speaker argues that operators should simply publish more accurate timetables to solve the problem.",
      "Bus bunching is caused by passengers who board too slowly, and the speaker recommends banning large bags on buses and removing bus lanes, which have been shown to make delays much worse.",
      "Although buses leave at regular intervals, traffic lights are the only cause of bunching, and operators have found that there is no practical way to keep buses evenly spaced along a route."],
     "A small delay grows as the delayed bus collects more passengers; operators have several remedies."),
    ("Paving you can feel",
     "The textured paving found at many pedestrian crossings and railway platforms was invented in Japan in the 1960s by Seiichi Miyake, who wanted to help a friend who was losing his sight. The first tiles were laid in the city of Okayama in 1967. The system uses two main patterns. Raised dots warn people with visual impairments that they are approaching a hazard, such as the edge of a platform or a road. Raised lines, running in the direction of travel, guide people along a safe route. The idea spread across Japan and then around the world, and in many countries it is now required by law in certain locations. Designers have had to balance different needs, however. The raised surfaces can be uncomfortable or difficult for some wheelchair users and people with walking difficulties, and inconsistent use of the patterns between cities can cause confusion.",
     "Tactile paving, invented in Japan in the 1960s, uses raised dots to warn of hazards and raised lines to guide direction. It has spread worldwide, but designers must consider wheelchair users and use the patterns consistently.",
     ["Tactile paving was invented in Europe to help wheelchair users cross roads safely, and it is now used only at railway stations, where its raised lines warn people about the edge of the platform.",
      "Tactile paving uses coloured tiles that people with poor vision can see more easily, and because every city uses exactly the same patterns, it has caused no problems for any group of users.",
      "The first textured tiles were laid in Okayama, but the idea was rejected elsewhere in Japan because the raised surfaces were considered too expensive to install and too difficult to clean regularly."],
     "Dots warn and lines guide; the design must balance the needs of different users."),
    ("Monderman's streets",
     "Most road safety measures add something: more signs, more barriers, more traffic lights. The Dutch traffic engineer Hans Monderman argued that in some places the opposite could work better. In several town centres in the Netherlands, he removed traffic lights, road markings, kerbs and signs, creating a shared space where cars, cyclists and pedestrians mix. His reasoning was that when drivers are unsure who has priority, they slow down and pay more attention to the people around them, making eye contact instead of watching signals. Supporters report that traffic in some of these places flows smoothly at low speeds and that the areas feel more pleasant. However, the approach has critics. Organisations representing blind and partially sighted people point out that kerbs and controlled crossings are essential for them, and that relying on eye contact excludes those who cannot make it.",
     "A Dutch engineer removed signs, lights and kerbs from some town centres, arguing that uncertainty makes drivers slow down and pay attention. Supporters praise the results, but organisations for blind people say the design excludes those who cannot rely on eye contact.",
     ["Hans Monderman added extra traffic lights and barriers to Dutch town centres, showing that more signs make drivers more careful, and organisations for blind people strongly supported his approach from the start.",
      "Shared space schemes separate cars from pedestrians with higher kerbs, and the speaker concludes that the design has been adopted by every city in the Netherlands without any criticism at all.",
      "Removing signs from town centres led to much higher speeds and more accidents, so the Netherlands abandoned shared space, although cyclists and pedestrians had enjoyed the more pleasant streets for a time."],
     "Uncertainty slows drivers, but blind people's organisations object."),
    ("Slowing traffic in towns",
     "The speed of a car has a dramatic effect on what happens to a pedestrian in a collision. At low speeds, most people who are hit survive; as speed increases, the risk of death rises steeply. For this reason, many towns use traffic calming measures in residential streets. Instead of relying only on speed limits and enforcement, these measures physically encourage drivers to slow down. They include road humps, raised crossings, narrowed sections of road and bends deliberately added to straight streets. Their great advantage is that they work all the time, without police present. They do have drawbacks. Road humps can slow down ambulances and fire engines, create noise as vehicles brake and accelerate, and push traffic onto neighbouring streets. Designers have responded with alternatives such as speed cushions, which have gaps wide enough for the wheels of large emergency vehicles to pass through without slowing.",
     "Because pedestrians are much more likely to die when hit at higher speeds, towns use physical measures such as humps and narrowed roads to slow traffic. These work constantly but can delay emergency vehicles, so designs such as speed cushions have been developed.",
     ["Road humps are mainly used to reduce noise in residential streets, and since police must be present for them to work, many towns have replaced them with lower speed limits instead.",
      "Traffic calming measures have no real drawbacks, because emergency vehicles are designed to pass over road humps at full speed, and drivers almost never choose different routes to avoid them.",
      "The speaker argues that the speed of a car makes little difference to pedestrian safety, and that the main purpose of traffic calming is to make residential streets look more attractive."],
     "Speed greatly affects survival; physical measures work but have drawbacks that designers address."),
    ("Standing on both sides",
     "On escalators in many cities, there is an unwritten rule: stand on one side and walk on the other. It seems efficient, but on very long escalators it may not be. In 2015 and 2016, London Underground ran a trial at Holborn station, where the escalators are unusually long. Staff asked passengers to stand on both sides and not walk. Because few people are willing to walk up a very tall escalator, the walking side had often been half empty, while queues built up at the bottom for the standing side. When everyone stood, the escalators carried significantly more people at busy times, and queues shrank. However, many passengers disliked being told not to walk, and some ignored the request. After the trial ended, most people returned to their old habits. The experiment showed that what feels fastest for an individual is not always best for the crowd.",
     "A London trial asked passengers to stand on both sides of very long escalators. This increased the number of people carried and reduced queues, but many passengers disliked it, and most returned to the usual habit afterwards.",
     ["London Underground found that walking on escalators always moves people faster, so it introduced a permanent rule requiring passengers to walk on both sides of the escalators at every station.",
      "The Holborn trial failed because standing on both sides created longer queues, and passengers were so pleased with the old system that the station decided never to repeat the experiment again.",
      "The trial showed that escalators at Holborn were too short for walking, and the main conclusion was that stations should build more stairs instead of relying on escalators during busy periods."],
     "Standing moved more people, but passengers disliked it and went back to walking."),
    ("Memos instead of slides",
     "At some companies, the most important meetings begin in silence. The online retailer Amazon became known for replacing slide presentations in senior meetings with written memos of up to about six pages. Instead of watching someone present, participants spend the first part of the meeting reading the memo quietly, and only then begin the discussion. The company's leaders argued that full sentences and paragraphs force the writer to think more clearly, because a narrative has to explain how ideas connect, whereas bullet points on slides can hide gaps in reasoning. Reading together also ensures that everyone starts the discussion with the same information, rather than pretending to have read a document in advance. The approach has critics. Writing a good memo takes a long time, and not every decision justifies that effort. Nevertheless, the idea has been copied by other organisations looking for better decisions.",
     "Some companies, including Amazon, replace slides with memos that everyone reads silently at the start of meetings. Supporters say full sentences force clearer thinking and give everyone the same information, although writing good memos takes a long time.",
     ["Amazon banned all written documents from its meetings because they took too long to read, and it now relies on short slide presentations with bullet points in order to speed up its decisions.",
      "The speaker argues that silent reading at the start of meetings wastes time, since most people read documents in advance, and that slides show how ideas connect more clearly than paragraphs.",
      "Six-page memos were introduced to reduce the number of meetings at Amazon, and critics say they make decisions too quickly because nobody has time to discuss the ideas they contain."],
     "Silent reading of narrative memos aims at clearer thinking, at the cost of writing time."),
    ("Ranking employees",
     "In the 1980s and 1990s, the American company General Electric became famous for a system of forced ranking. Managers had to place employees into categories: roughly the top twenty percent, who were rewarded, the middle seventy percent, and the bottom ten percent, who were often asked to leave. Supporters argued that the system prevented managers from giving everyone the same average rating and ensured that poor performance was addressed. Many other large companies adopted similar methods. Over time, however, criticism grew. Because employees were compared with their colleagues, the system could discourage cooperation, since helping a teammate might lower your own ranking. It could also be unfair in a strong team, where someone had to be labelled a poor performer even if everyone was doing well. Many companies, including some early adopters, have since abandoned forced ranking in favour of more frequent conversations about performance.",
     "Forced ranking, popularised by General Electric, required managers to sort staff into fixed categories, with the lowest group often dismissed. It was widely copied, but it discouraged cooperation and could be unfair to strong teams, so many companies have dropped it.",
     ["General Electric introduced forced ranking to encourage teamwork, and the system proved so successful at improving cooperation that almost every large company still uses it in exactly the same form.",
      "Under forced ranking, every employee received the same average rating, which critics said hid poor performance, so companies replaced it with a system that dismissed the lowest ten percent of staff each year.",
      "Forced ranking was abandoned because it was too expensive to run, although employees generally preferred it to regular conversations with their managers about how well they were doing in their jobs."],
     "Fixed categories were widely copied but hurt cooperation, so many firms abandoned them."),
    ("Does brainstorming work?",
     "The term brainstorming was popularised in the 1950s by the advertising executive Alex Osborn, who claimed that groups following his rules, such as withholding criticism and aiming for quantity, could produce far more ideas than individuals. Later research has not supported this claim. In many experiments, people who worked alone and then pooled their ideas produced more, and often better, ideas than the same number of people brainstorming together. Psychologists have identified several reasons. In a group, only one person can speak at a time, so others may forget their ideas while waiting. Some people hold back because they worry about being judged, and some put in less effort when others can carry the load. Techniques such as brainwriting, in which people write ideas down individually before sharing them, try to keep the benefits of group discussion while avoiding these problems.",
     "Although brainstorming was promoted as a way for groups to generate more ideas, research shows individuals working alone and then pooling ideas often do better. Waiting to speak and fear of judgement are reasons, and brainwriting tries to avoid these problems.",
     ["Research has confirmed Alex Osborn's claim that groups produce more ideas than individuals, mainly because group members encourage each other to speak and nobody worries about being judged by others.",
      "Brainstorming fails because groups criticise ideas too harshly, so psychologists recommend banning all discussion and asking a single expert to make every decision alone, without consulting any of their colleagues at all.",
      "The speaker explains that brainwriting was invented in the 1950s by an advertising executive, who found that people write faster than they speak and therefore produce better ideas in groups."],
     "Research contradicts Osborn's claim; group problems explain why, and brainwriting responds."),
    ("Stopping the production line",
     "On a Toyota production line, any worker who notices a problem can pull a cord, called an andon cord, that alerts a team leader and, if the problem cannot be solved quickly, stops the entire line. To outsiders this seems surprising. Stopping production is expensive, and many factories had traditionally tried to keep lines moving at all costs, fixing faulty products at the end. Toyota's approach was based on the idea that problems should be solved immediately, at their source, before faults are repeated on many vehicles. It also sent a clear message that every worker was trusted and responsible for quality. The system became one of the best-known features of Toyota's production methods. Other industries have borrowed the idea. Some hospitals, for example, encourage any member of staff, regardless of seniority, to stop a procedure if they believe a patient's safety is at risk.",
     "Toyota allows any worker to pull a cord that can stop the production line when a problem appears, so faults are fixed at their source. This shows trust in workers, and similar ideas have been adopted elsewhere, including in hospitals.",
     ["Toyota prevents workers from stopping the production line because it is too expensive, preferring to fix faulty cars at the end, and hospitals have copied this approach to avoid delays in treatment.",
      "The andon cord allows managers to stop production when they are dissatisfied with workers, and the system was abandoned after other car companies showed that it reduced quality across their factories.",
      "The speaker explains that the andon cord was first used in hospitals to protect patients and was later adopted by Toyota, where only senior engineers are allowed to use it on the line."],
     "Any worker can stop the line so problems are fixed at once; hospitals borrowed the idea."),
    ("Alfa, Bravo, Charlie",
     "Over a noisy radio connection, letters such as B, D and P can sound almost identical, and a single misunderstood letter can cause serious problems, for example in an aircraft's call sign. For this reason, pilots and air traffic controllers use a spelling alphabet in which each letter is represented by a word: Alfa, Bravo, Charlie and so on. The current version was adopted internationally in the 1950s after extensive testing. The words were chosen so that they would be clearly distinguishable from one another and easy to pronounce for speakers of different languages, not only English. Some spellings were adjusted for this reason: Alfa is written with an f, and Juliett with two t's, so that speakers of other languages would pronounce them correctly. The alphabet is now used well beyond aviation, by the military, police, emergency services and customer service staff.",
     "Because letters can be confused over the radio, aviation uses a spelling alphabet with a word for each letter. Adopted in the 1950s after testing, its words were chosen to be distinct and easy for speakers of many languages to pronounce.",
     ["The spelling alphabet was designed for English speakers only, and it is now used mainly in customer service because pilots prefer to spell out letters without using any code words.",
      "Pilots once used numbers instead of letters over the radio, and the spelling alphabet was introduced in the 1990s after a series of accidents caused by confusing numbers that sounded similar.",
      "The alphabet's unusual spellings, such as Alfa, are mistakes that were never corrected, and experts are now considering replacing the words with ones that are easier for English speakers to remember."],
     "The words prevent confusion and were chosen for speakers of many languages."),
    ("The meaning of SOS",
     "The distress signal SOS was adopted at an international radio conference in 1906 and came into use in 1908. Many people believe the letters stand for save our souls or save our ship, but these phrases were invented afterwards. SOS was chosen because in Morse code it forms a simple, distinctive pattern: three dots, three dashes, three dots, sent as one continuous signal. It is easy to transmit and difficult to mistake for anything else, even by an operator with poor reception. Before SOS became universal, different countries used different signals, which could cause confusion. When the Titanic sank in 1912, its radio operators sent both the older signal and SOS. By the end of the twentieth century, Morse code had been replaced at sea by satellite-based distress systems, which can automatically send a ship's position, but SOS remains widely recognised as a call for help.",
     "SOS was chosen as a distress signal because its Morse pattern is simple and hard to mistake, not because the letters stand for a phrase. It replaced varied national signals, and although satellites have replaced Morse at sea, SOS remains widely recognised.",
     ["SOS originally stood for save our souls, and it was chosen by the Titanic's radio operators in 1912 because the older distress signal had already been abolished by international agreement.",
      "The letters SOS were selected because they were the easiest to pronounce over the radio, and Morse code is still the main system that ships use to call for help at sea today.",
      "Different countries continued to use their own distress signals until the end of the twentieth century, when satellite systems introduced SOS as the first international call for help at sea."],
     "The pattern, not a phrase, explains SOS; it outlived Morse code at sea."),
    ("James Biggs's white stick",
     "The white cane is recognised in many countries as a symbol of blindness, but its history is relatively recent. In 1921, James Biggs, an English photographer who had lost his sight, painted his walking stick white so that drivers would notice him more easily as traffic increased. In the 1930s, organisations in France and North America campaigned for white canes to be widely adopted, and many places passed laws requiring drivers to give way to pedestrians carrying one. The cane's role changed significantly after the Second World War, when a rehabilitation specialist working with blinded veterans developed a technique using a longer cane. Instead of simply supporting the user or signalling to others, it was swept from side to side in front of the body to detect obstacles, steps and kerbs. This long cane technique is still taught today, alongside guide dogs and newer electronic aids.",
     "The white cane began in 1921 when a blind photographer painted his stick white to be seen by drivers. Campaigns and laws spread its use, and after the Second World War a longer cane was developed to detect obstacles, a technique still taught.",
     ["White canes were first introduced for soldiers blinded in the First World War, and they are now used mainly as walking supports rather than as a way of detecting obstacles in the street.",
      "The long cane technique was developed in the 1920s by a photographer, and it has since been completely replaced by guide dogs and electronic devices in most countries around the world.",
      "Laws in the 1930s banned white canes because drivers found them confusing, but they returned after the war when veterans campaigned for the right to use them again in public places."],
     "From visibility in 1921 to the long cane technique after the war."),
    ("Headphones and hearing",
     "Listening to music through headphones is one of the most common ways people expose themselves to loud sound. Inside the inner ear, tiny hair cells convert sound vibrations into signals for the brain. Very loud or prolonged noise can damage these cells, and in humans they do not grow back, so the resulting hearing loss is permanent. Often it develops gradually, and people may not notice until it is advanced. The risk depends on both volume and time: a moderately loud sound can be harmful if heard for many hours, while a very loud sound can cause damage quickly. The World Health Organization has warned that more than a billion young people are at risk because of unsafe listening habits. Some experts suggest a simple guideline: listen at no more than sixty percent of maximum volume for no more than sixty minutes at a time.",
     "Loud or prolonged sound through headphones can permanently damage the hair cells in the inner ear, often without people noticing. Risk depends on both volume and time, so experts suggest limits such as sixty percent volume for sixty minutes.",
     ["Hearing loss from headphones is usually temporary, because the hair cells in the inner ear grow back within a few weeks, so doctors recommend occasional breaks rather than a lower volume.",
      "Only very loud sounds can damage hearing, while moderate volumes are safe for any length of time, so experts say young people can listen for as long as they like at medium volume.",
      "The World Health Organization has banned headphones for young people because more than a billion have already lost their hearing completely through unsafe listening habits over the last few years."],
     "Damage is permanent and depends on both volume and duration."),
    ("Mediterranean eating",
     "The Mediterranean diet, rich in vegetables, fruit, whole grains, fish and olive oil, has long been associated with good health. One of the most influential studies was a large trial in Spain involving around seven and a half thousand people at high risk of heart disease. Participants were randomly assigned either to a Mediterranean diet with extra olive oil or nuts, or to advice to follow a low-fat diet. When the results were published in 2013, the Mediterranean groups had significantly fewer heart attacks, strokes and deaths from heart disease. Later, however, researchers discovered problems with how some participants had been assigned to groups. The original paper was withdrawn in 2018 and republished with a reanalysis. The main conclusions were similar, but the authors described them more cautiously. The episode shows both the value of large trials and the importance of checking how they are carried out.",
     "A large Spanish trial found that people at high risk of heart disease had fewer heart problems on a Mediterranean diet. Problems with group assignment led to the paper being withdrawn and republished with similar but more cautious conclusions.",
     ["A Spanish trial showed that a low-fat diet protects the heart better than a Mediterranean diet, and the study was withdrawn because its results could not be repeated in other countries.",
      "The Mediterranean diet trial was cancelled in 2013 after participants refused to eat olive oil and nuts, and researchers concluded that diet has no effect on the risk of heart disease.",
      "Although the trial's methods were flawed, the reanalysis found that the Mediterranean diet caused more strokes, so doctors now advise against it for people at high risk of heart disease."],
     "The benefit held up after reanalysis, but was stated more cautiously."),
    ("Cutting sodium slowly",
     "Eating too much salt raises blood pressure, which increases the risk of heart attacks and strokes. Much of the salt people eat is not added at the table but is already in processed foods such as bread, cereals and ready meals. In the early 2000s, the United Kingdom began a programme of salt reduction that worked with food manufacturers to lower the salt content of their products gradually. The key was to make changes slowly, a little at a time, so that customers did not notice a difference in taste and did not switch to other brands. Over the following decade, average salt consumption fell noticeably, and researchers linked this to falls in average blood pressure. Because the targets were voluntary, however, progress depended on the cooperation of the industry, and some experts believe it slowed when government pressure weakened in later years.",
     "The United Kingdom worked with food manufacturers to reduce salt in processed foods gradually, so customers did not notice. Salt intake and blood pressure fell, but because targets were voluntary, progress slowed when pressure on the industry weakened.",
     ["The United Kingdom reduced salt consumption by banning salt shakers in restaurants, since most salt is added at the table, and blood pressure fell as a result of this strict new law.",
      "Food manufacturers cut salt quickly and dramatically, which caused many customers to switch brands, so the government abandoned its salt reduction programme after only a few years of disappointing sales and complaints.",
      "The salt reduction programme had no measurable effect on health, and experts believe voluntary targets are more effective than laws because companies always cooperate fully with them over long periods."],
     "Gradual, voluntary reductions worked but slowed when pressure eased."),
    ("Fluoridation",
     "In the early twentieth century, a dentist in Colorado noticed that many local people had brown stains on their teeth, but surprisingly few cavities. Researchers eventually linked both effects to naturally high levels of fluoride in the water supply. Fluoride makes tooth enamel more resistant to decay, but too much can cause staining. In 1945, the city of Grand Rapids in the United States became the first to add a carefully controlled amount of fluoride to its drinking water, and rates of tooth decay among children fell. Many countries followed. The practice remains debated. Critics argue that people should be able to choose whether to consume fluoride, and that the benefit is now smaller because most toothpaste contains it. Several European countries do not fluoridate their water. However, major public health organisations continue to support fluoridation at recommended levels as a safe and cheap way to reduce tooth decay.",
     "The link between fluoride and fewer cavities was discovered through stained teeth in Colorado, and cities began adding controlled amounts to water from 1945. The practice is debated, partly because toothpaste now contains fluoride, but health organisations still support it.",
     ["Fluoride was added to water in Colorado because local people had many cavities, and it has since been banned worldwide after studies showed that it causes tooth decay in children.",
      "Grand Rapids stopped adding fluoride to its water in 1945 because of brown stains on teeth, and most European countries now require fluoride in every public water supply by law.",
      "Public health organisations no longer recommend fluoridation, because toothpaste has made it unnecessary, although critics argue that governments should add even more fluoride to drinking water than they currently do."],
     "Discovered by accident, adopted from 1945, debated today but still supported."),
    ("Risk compensation",
     "When cars become safer, do drivers behave more carefully or less? A well-known study in Munich in the 1980s followed a fleet of taxis, some fitted with anti-lock brakes, which help drivers keep control when braking hard, and some without. The researchers expected the taxis with the new brakes to have fewer accidents. They did not. Observers found that drivers of the better-equipped taxis tended to follow other cars more closely and brake more sharply. This is an example of what psychologists call risk compensation: when people feel safer, they may take more risks, cancelling out some of the benefit. The idea has been used to question safety measures from seat belts to cycle helmets. However, most researchers do not conclude that safety features are useless. Rather, they argue that the real effect of a safety measure depends on how people change their behaviour in response.",
     "A Munich study found taxis with anti-lock brakes had no fewer accidents, because their drivers followed closer and braked harder. This illustrates risk compensation, though researchers argue safety features still matter and their effect depends on how behaviour changes.",
     ["Taxis fitted with anti-lock brakes had far fewer accidents in Munich, which shows that drivers become more careful when their vehicles are safer, exactly as psychologists had predicted in advance.",
      "Risk compensation proves that seat belts, helmets and anti-lock brakes make roads more dangerous, so researchers recommend removing these features from vehicles to encourage careful driving among ordinary motorists.",
      "The Munich study compared taxi drivers with private drivers and found that professional drivers take fewer risks, because they are trained to keep a safe distance from other vehicles at all times."],
     "Safer equipment led to riskier driving, yet safety features are not considered useless."),
    ("The Plimsoll line",
     "In the nineteenth century, many British cargo ships sailed dangerously overloaded, and hundreds were lost at sea each year. Some owners had little incentive to prevent this, because their ships and cargoes were insured, while the sailors who drowned were easily replaced. The politician Samuel Plimsoll campaigned against these so-called coffin ships, facing strong opposition from shipowners. In 1876, a law was passed requiring ships to carry a mark on their hull showing the maximum depth to which they could be loaded. This became known as the Plimsoll line. Today load lines are required by international agreement. They include several marks, because a ship floats at a different depth depending on the water: it sits lower in fresh water than in denser seawater, and extra safety margins are needed in winter seas. The simple painted line has saved countless lives.",
     "Overloaded British ships once sank in large numbers, partly because owners were insured. After Samuel Plimsoll's campaign, an 1876 law required a load line on hulls, and international rules now include several marks for different waters and seasons.",
     ["Samuel Plimsoll was a shipowner who introduced the load line to protect his cargoes, and the law was passed quickly because other shipowners strongly supported his campaign from the very beginning.",
      "The Plimsoll line shows the minimum depth a ship must reach before it can sail, and there is only one mark because ships float at exactly the same depth in all kinds of water.",
      "Load lines were introduced in 1876 to reduce insurance costs for shipowners, and they have since been replaced by electronic sensors that measure the weight of the cargo on board directly."],
     "A campaign against overloading led to the load line, now with several marks."),
    ("Where the date changes",
     "The International Date Line runs roughly along the one hundred and eightieth meridian, through the Pacific Ocean, and marks where one calendar day ends and the next begins. A traveller crossing it towards the west moves forward a day, while crossing towards the east moves back a day. The need for such a line was noticed centuries ago. When the survivors of Ferdinand Magellan's expedition returned to Spain in 1522 after sailing around the world westwards, they discovered that their carefully kept log was one day behind the calendar at home. The date line is not straight. It zigzags so that island groups and countries are not divided between two dates. Countries can even choose which side to be on. In 2011, Samoa moved to the other side of the line, skipping the thirtieth of December entirely, so that its working week would match those of its main trading partners, Australia and New Zealand.",
     "The International Date Line, running roughly through the middle of the Pacific, marks where one day changes to the next. It bends around countries, which can choose their side, as Samoa did in 2011 to match its trading partners.",
     ["The date line follows a perfectly straight path along the one hundred and eightieth meridian, and no country is allowed to change sides, because this would disrupt international trade and travel.",
      "Magellan's expedition created the International Date Line in 1522 after its sailors lost a day, and crossing the line towards the east moves a traveller forward to the next day.",
      "Samoa moved to the other side of the date line to become one day behind Australia, which made trade easier, and it repeated the change several times in the following years."],
     "The line marks the change of date, bends around countries, and Samoa switched sides."),
    ("Finding longitude at sea",
     "For centuries, sailors could measure their latitude from the Sun and stars, but longitude, their position east or west, was far harder to determine. Mistakes caused shipwrecks and lost cargoes. One solution was to carry an accurate clock showing the time at the home port: comparing it with local noon at sea revealed how far east or west the ship had travelled. The problem was that ordinary clocks lost accuracy on a moving ship. In 1714, the British government offered a large prize for a practical solution. John Harrison, a largely self-taught clockmaker, spent decades building a series of sea clocks. His fourth design, the size of a large pocket watch, performed remarkably well on a voyage to Jamaica in 1761. Yet the officials responsible, many of whom favoured an astronomical method, were reluctant to pay him in full. Only after years of dispute, and the involvement of the king, did he receive most of the money.",
     "Finding longitude at sea required an accurate clock showing home time. John Harrison spent decades building sea clocks, and his fourth performed very well in 1761, but officials who favoured astronomical methods delayed paying him most of the prize.",
     ["Harrison won the longitude prize immediately in 1714 by inventing a method of measuring latitude from the stars, which officials praised as far more practical than using clocks at sea.",
      "Sailors could already find longitude easily in the eighteenth century, and Harrison's clocks were designed mainly to measure latitude, a problem that had caused a great many shipwrecks and lost cargoes.",
      "Harrison's first sea clock was small enough to fit in a pocket and worked perfectly, but he refused the prize money because he preferred to keep his clever design secret from others."],
     "An accurate clock solved longitude, but Harrison struggled to be paid."),
    ("The seven-day cycle",
     "Unlike the day, the month and the year, the seven-day week is not based directly on any single natural cycle. Its origins are debated, but historians point to several influences. Ancient Babylonians gave special importance to the number seven, and seven celestial bodies could be seen moving across the sky: the Sun, the Moon and five planets. A quarter of a lunar month is also roughly seven days. The Jewish tradition of a weekly day of rest spread the seven-day week further, and it was eventually adopted throughout the Roman world. Many languages still link the days to those celestial bodies: in English, Sunday and Monday refer to the Sun and the Moon, and Saturday to Saturn. The week has proved remarkably resistant to change. Revolutionary France tried a ten-day week, and the Soviet Union experimented with five- and six-day weeks, but both attempts were abandoned.",
     "The seven-day week has uncertain origins, possibly linked to seven visible celestial bodies, lunar phases and the Jewish day of rest. Many day names still refer to those bodies, and attempts by France and the Soviet Union to change it failed.",
     ["The seven-day week is based directly on the time the Moon takes to orbit the Earth, and every culture in history has always used exactly the same week without any variation at all.",
      "Revolutionary France and the Soviet Union successfully replaced the seven-day week, and most modern countries now use a ten-day week based on the metric system introduced at that time.",
      "English day names come from Roman emperors rather than from the Sun, Moon and planets, and the seven-day week was invented by the Romans to organise military campaigns across Europe."],
     "Several influences shaped the week, and attempts to change it failed."),
    ("A number for nothing",
     "It is easy to take zero for granted, but for much of history it did not exist as a number. Some ancient civilisations, such as the Babylonians, used a symbol as a placeholder to show an empty position in a number, rather like the gap that distinguishes two hundred and five from twenty-five. However, they did not treat it as a number in its own right. In India, mathematicians went further. In the seventh century, the mathematician Brahmagupta set out rules for calculating with zero, such as the result of adding zero to a number or subtracting a number from itself. Indian numerals, including zero, spread to the Islamic world, where scholars developed them further, and eventually reached Europe. There they were adopted only slowly, but the place-value system they made possible is the foundation of modern mathematics and computing.",
     "Zero was first used by some civilisations only as a placeholder. Indian mathematicians, including Brahmagupta in the seventh century, treated it as a number with rules, and the system spread through the Islamic world to Europe, becoming the basis of modern mathematics.",
     ["The Babylonians invented zero as a number and set out the rules for calculating with it, and Europeans adopted the system immediately because it was far simpler than their own.",
      "Zero was invented in Europe in the seventh century and later spread to India, where mathematicians used it only as a placeholder rather than as a number in its own right.",
      "Brahmagupta rejected the idea of zero because it caused errors in calculations, so modern computers use a different system that does not depend on the position of digits in a number."],
     "From placeholder to number in India, then to the Islamic world and Europe."),
    ("Fibonacci's book of calculation",
     "In 1202, the Italian mathematician Leonardo of Pisa, now known as Fibonacci, published a book called Liber Abaci. Having grown up partly in North Africa, where his father worked as a trade official, he had learned the numerals used by Arab merchants, which had originated in India. His book showed European readers how these numerals made practical calculations, such as converting currencies and calculating interest, far easier than with Roman numerals. The book also contains a puzzle about breeding rabbits that produces the sequence of numbers now named after him, although the sequence had been described centuries earlier by Indian mathematicians. The new numerals were not accepted everywhere at once. In 1299, the city of Florence banned them in official accounts, partly because they were easier to alter fraudulently, for example by turning a zero into a six or nine.",
     "Fibonacci's 1202 book introduced Indian-Arabic numerals to Europe, showing how they simplified practical calculations. It includes the sequence named after him, described earlier in India, and adoption was slow, with Florence banning the numerals in official accounts.",
     ["Fibonacci invented the numerals used today while living in North Africa, and Florence quickly made them compulsory in all official accounts because they were impossible to alter fraudulently in any way.",
      "Liber Abaci was mainly a book about breeding rabbits, and the number sequence it describes was discovered by Fibonacci for the first time, without any earlier examples from other mathematicians.",
      "Fibonacci argued that Roman numerals were better for converting currencies and calculating interest, and his book persuaded European merchants to keep using them for several more centuries in their trade."],
     "The book spread new numerals, but acceptance was slow."),
    ("Sixty minutes in an hour",
     "Why are there sixty minutes in an hour and sixty seconds in a minute, when most of our counting is based on tens? The answer goes back to ancient Mesopotamia, where the Babylonians used a number system based on sixty. Sixty is a convenient number because it can be divided evenly in many ways: by two, three, four, five, six, ten, twelve, fifteen, twenty and thirty. This made fractions much easier to handle, especially in astronomy, and it also explains the three hundred and sixty degrees in a circle. The division of the day into twenty-four hours is usually traced to ancient Egypt, where daytime and night were each divided into twelve parts. Attempts to change these units have failed. During the French Revolution, clocks showing ten hours a day, each of one hundred minutes, were introduced, but the system was abandoned after only a couple of years.",
     "Our minutes and seconds come from the Babylonian number system based on sixty, which divides evenly in many ways and also gave us 360 degrees. The twenty-four-hour day comes from Egypt, and France's attempt at decimal time was quickly abandoned.",
     ["The Babylonians chose sixty because it is a multiple of ten, and the twenty-four-hour day was introduced during the French Revolution, replacing an older ten-hour system used across Europe.",
      "The number sixty was chosen because it is difficult to divide, which prevented errors in astronomy, and decimal clocks are still widely used in France for many official purposes today.",
      "Ancient Egyptians divided the hour into sixty minutes, while the Babylonians invented the twenty-four-hour day, and both systems were later replaced by decimal time in most of Europe for centuries."],
     "Base sixty from Babylon, twenty-four hours from Egypt, decimal time abandoned."),
    ("Prime numbers and online security",
     "Every time you buy something online, your payment details are protected by encryption, and some of the most widely used methods rely on prime numbers. Multiplying two large prime numbers together is easy for a computer, even if each has hundreds of digits. But reversing the process, taking the result and working out which two primes produced it, is extraordinarily difficult. With current computers, it could take an impractically long time. A system published in 1977, known as RSA after the initials of its inventors, uses this difference. It allows a website to publish a public key that anyone can use to lock a message, while only the owner of a matching private key can unlock it. Researchers are concerned that future quantum computers could solve the factoring problem much faster, which is why new encryption methods designed to resist them are already being developed and introduced.",
     "Some encryption relies on the fact that multiplying large primes is easy but finding the original primes from the result is extremely hard. RSA uses this for public and private keys, but quantum computers could threaten it, so new methods are being developed.",
     ["Encryption works because dividing large numbers by primes is impossible for computers, and quantum computers are expected to make current methods even more secure in the future than they are today.",
      "The RSA system uses a single secret key that is shared by the website and its customers, and it relies on the fact that multiplying prime numbers takes computers a very long time.",
      "Prime numbers are no longer used in online payments because researchers found a quick way to factor large numbers in 1977, which led directly to the development of quantum computers."],
     "Easy multiplication and hard factoring underpin RSA, which quantum computers may threaten."),
    ("Three doors and a car",
     "Imagine a game show with three closed doors. Behind one is a car; behind the other two are goats. You choose a door. The host, who knows what is behind each door, then opens one of the doors you did not choose, always revealing a goat, and asks whether you want to switch to the remaining closed door. Most people feel it makes no difference: two doors are left, so the chance must be fifty-fifty. In fact, switching doubles your chance of winning, from one in three to two in three. Your first choice had only a one-in-three chance of being right, and the host's action does not change that; if you were wrong, the car must be behind the other door. When a magazine columnist explained this in 1990, she received thousands of letters insisting she was mistaken, including some from people with doctorates. Computer simulations have since confirmed the answer many times.",
     "In the Monty Hall problem, a contestant who switches doors after the host reveals a goat wins two times in three, not half the time. Many readers, including highly educated ones, rejected this answer in 1990, but simulations have repeatedly confirmed it.",
     ["Once the host opens a door, the chance of winning is exactly fifty-fifty, so switching makes no difference, as a magazine columnist correctly explained to her many readers in her column in 1990.",
      "Switching doors in the game reduces the chance of winning the car, because the host deliberately opens the door hiding the prize, which misleads most contestants into changing their original choice.",
      "The Monty Hall problem shows that contestants who stay with their first choice win two times in three, and computer simulations have shown that mathematicians were right to criticise the columnist."],
     "Switching wins two times in three, despite widespread disbelief."),
    ("Shared birthdays",
     "How many people need to be in a room before there is a better than even chance that two of them share a birthday? Many people guess around one hundred and eighty, half the number of days in a year. The real answer is just twenty-three. With seventy people, a shared birthday is almost certain. The result seems surprising because we tend to think about the chance that someone shares our own birthday, which is low. But the question is about any two people in the room. Among twenty-three people, there are two hundred and fifty-three possible pairs, and each pair is a chance for a match. The same mathematics matters in computer security. Systems that produce short digital fingerprints for files can be attacked by searching for two different files with the same fingerprint, which is much easier than matching one particular file, a technique known as a birthday attack.",
     "Only twenty-three people are needed for an even chance that two share a birthday, because the question concerns any pair, and there are many pairs. The same principle explains birthday attacks, which search for any two files with matching digital fingerprints.",
     ["At least one hundred and eighty people are needed before two are likely to share a birthday, and computer systems use this fact to make digital fingerprints impossible for attackers to copy.",
      "The birthday paradox shows that the chance of someone sharing your own birthday is very high in a group of twenty-three, which is why birthday attacks are aimed at individual people.",
      "With seventy people in a room there is only a small chance of a shared birthday, and the paradox has no practical use outside mathematics classrooms and books of puzzles."],
     "Many possible pairs make a match likely; the idea applies to computer security."),
    ("The first-digit pattern",
     "If you look at the first digits of numbers in many real-world collections of data, such as populations of towns, lengths of rivers or company accounts, you might expect each digit from one to nine to appear equally often. Surprisingly, it does not. The digit one appears first about thirty percent of the time, while nine appears first less than five percent of the time. This pattern is known as Benford's law. It was first noticed in the nineteenth century by an astronomer who observed that the early pages of books of logarithm tables, used for numbers beginning with one, were more worn than later pages. The law has a practical use. When people invent numbers, they tend to spread the first digits too evenly, so auditors sometimes check whether financial figures follow the expected pattern. A mismatch does not prove fraud, but it can suggest where to look more closely.",
     "In many real data sets, numbers begin with the digit one far more often than with nine, a pattern called Benford's law. Because invented figures rarely follow it, auditors use it to find accounts that deserve closer investigation, though not as proof of fraud.",
     ["Benford's law states that each digit from one to nine appears first equally often in real data, so auditors look carefully for numbers that begin too often with the digit one.",
      "The pattern was discovered by an auditor who noticed that fraudsters always choose the digit nine, and any mismatch with Benford's law is now accepted as legal proof of fraud.",
      "Benford's law applies only to numbers invented by people, which is why logarithm tables showed more wear on their later pages, and it has no use in checking financial figures."],
     "Low first digits dominate real data, which helps auditors spot invented figures."),
    ("The missing bullet holes",
     "During the Second World War, analysts studied bombers returning from missions and recorded where they had been hit. Bullet holes were concentrated on the wings and the body of the aircraft, while the engines showed relatively few. An obvious conclusion was to add armour where the holes were most common. The statistician Abraham Wald pointed out a flaw in this reasoning. The analysts were only looking at planes that had survived. Aircraft hit in the engines were less likely to return at all, so the lack of holes there did not mean the engines were rarely hit; it meant that hits there were deadly. The armour, he argued, should go where the returning planes had no damage. This mistake is called survivorship bias, and it appears in many areas. For example, studying only successful companies can make risky strategies look safer than they really are, because the failures are no longer visible.",
     "Analysts wanted to armour bombers where returning planes had most bullet holes, but Abraham Wald noted that planes hit elsewhere, such as the engines, had not returned. This error, survivorship bias, also affects conclusions drawn from studying only successful companies.",
     ["Abraham Wald recommended adding armour to the wings and body of bombers, where most bullet holes were found, because these areas were clearly the most vulnerable to enemy fire during missions.",
      "Survivorship bias means that successful companies always use safer strategies than failed ones, and Wald showed this by studying aircraft engines that had been repaired after the war in military factories.",
      "The engines of returning bombers were covered in bullet holes, so analysts concluded that engines needed extra armour, a decision that Wald said was based on complete data from every mission."],
     "Only survivors were studied, so the missing damage pointed to the real weak spots."),
    ("Ice cream and drowning",
     "In many places, ice cream sales and the number of drownings rise and fall together throughout the year. Does eating ice cream cause people to drown? Of course not. Both increase in hot weather, when more people buy ice cream and more people go swimming. This is a classic example of the difference between correlation and causation. Two things can move together because a third factor, sometimes called a confounding variable, affects both of them. Sometimes the link is pure coincidence; sometimes the direction of cause is reversed. For this reason, scientists prefer randomised experiments, in which people are assigned to different groups by chance, so that other factors are spread evenly between them. When experiments are impossible or unethical, researchers must use observational data with great care, trying to account for possible confounding factors before drawing conclusions about cause.",
     "Ice cream sales and drownings rise together because hot weather affects both, showing that correlation does not prove causation. Randomised experiments help rule out such hidden factors, and when they are impossible, observational data must be interpreted with great care.",
     ["Research has shown that eating ice cream increases the risk of drowning, which is why scientists recommend observational studies rather than randomised experiments when studying the causes of accidents at the beach.",
      "Correlations between two things are always coincidences, so scientists ignore them completely and rely only on experiments that assign people to groups according to their own personal preferences and daily habits.",
      "Hot weather reduces both ice cream sales and swimming, and the speaker argues that confounding variables are rare, so observational data can usually be trusted without special care or checking."],
     "A third factor explains the link, which is why randomised experiments are preferred."),
    ("Regression to the mean",
     "When a result is unusually good or unusually bad, the next result is likely to be closer to average, simply because extreme results usually involve some luck. This is called regression to the mean, and it can easily lead to false conclusions. The psychologist Daniel Kahneman described flight instructors who believed that praising a trainee after an excellent landing made the next landing worse, while criticising a poor landing made the next one better. In fact, the changes would have happened anyway: after an exceptional performance, an average one is more likely to follow. The same effect can make policies look more successful than they are. Speed cameras, for example, are often installed at locations after an unusually high number of accidents. If accidents then fall, part of the fall may be due to regression to the mean rather than the cameras, so careful studies compare these sites with similar ones.",
     "Extreme results are usually followed by more average ones because luck plays a part, a pattern called regression to the mean. It misled flight instructors about criticism and can exaggerate the success of measures such as speed cameras installed after accident spikes.",
     ["Kahneman showed that criticising trainee pilots improves their landings while praise makes them worse, which proves that flight instructors should never encourage their students, even after very good performances in training.",
      "Regression to the mean means that unusually good results are usually followed by even better ones, so speed cameras always cause large falls in accidents on the roads where they are installed.",
      "Speed cameras are placed randomly across road networks to avoid regression to the mean, and studies comparing sites show that luck has no effect whatsoever on accident numbers over time."],
     "Luck makes extremes fade, which can mislead judgements about praise and policies."),
    ("The horse that could count",
     "In the early twentieth century, a horse in Germany called Clever Hans appeared to solve arithmetic problems, tapping his hoof to give the answer. Investigators eventually discovered that the horse was responding to tiny, unconscious changes in the posture and facial expressions of the people around him, who knew the answer. The case illustrates why modern medical trials are often double-blind. In such a trial, neither the participants nor the researchers who assess them know who is receiving the real treatment and who is receiving a placebo or a comparison treatment. If participants know, their expectations can affect how they feel and what they report. If researchers know, they may unintentionally treat patients differently or interpret results more favourably. By keeping everyone unaware until the results are analysed, double-blind trials help ensure that any difference between the groups is caused by the treatment itself.",
     "The story of a horse responding to people's unconscious cues shows how expectations affect results. In double-blind trials neither participants nor assessing researchers know who receives the real treatment, helping ensure differences are caused by the treatment itself.",
     ["Clever Hans could genuinely solve arithmetic problems, and his case showed that animals should be used in medical trials instead of human participants, whose expectations affect results in many ways.",
      "In a double-blind trial, participants choose whether to receive the real treatment or a placebo, while researchers know exactly which patients are in each group from the start of the study.",
      "Double-blind trials were introduced because researchers deliberately changed results, and the speaker argues that participants' expectations have no effect at all on how they feel or what they report."],
     "Unconscious cues explain Clever Hans; blinding stops expectations shaping results."),
    ("Repeating psychology studies",
     "In 2015, a large group of researchers published the results of an ambitious project. They had tried to repeat one hundred studies published in leading psychology journals, following the original methods as closely as possible. Only around a third to two fifths of the repeated studies produced clearly similar results. The project drew attention to what became known as the replication crisis. Researchers identified several contributing factors. Many original studies used small samples, which make chance findings more likely. Journals tended to publish surprising positive results rather than studies that found nothing, and researchers sometimes tried many different ways of analysing their data until one produced an interesting result. The problem is not limited to psychology. In response, many scientists now register their plans before collecting data, use larger samples and share their data, so that others can check their work more easily.",
     "A 2015 project repeated one hundred psychology studies and found that only a minority produced similar results. Small samples, a preference for surprising findings and flexible analysis contributed, and researchers now register plans, use larger samples and share data.",
     ["A 2015 project found that nearly all psychology studies could be repeated successfully, which showed that concerns about small samples and publication practices had been greatly exaggerated by earlier critics.",
      "The replication crisis affects only psychology, and it was caused by researchers sharing their data too freely, so scientists now keep their data private until the results are published in journals.",
      "Journals have always preferred to publish studies that found nothing, and the speaker argues that larger samples make chance findings more likely, so smaller studies are now recommended by experts."],
     "Few studies replicated; causes were identified and practices have changed."),
    ("Alfred Nobel's fortune",
     "Alfred Nobel, the Swedish chemist who invented dynamite, made a fortune from explosives. According to a well-known story, in 1888 a French newspaper mistakenly published his obituary when it was actually his brother who had died, and described him as a merchant of death who had grown rich by finding ways to kill people faster. Historians are not certain how much this episode influenced him, but in his will, signed in 1895, Nobel left most of his fortune to establish prizes for those who had conferred the greatest benefit on humankind in physics, chemistry, medicine, literature and peace. The first prizes were awarded in 1901. A prize in economics was added much later, in 1968, funded by Sweden's central bank in Nobel's memory, which is why it is officially not one of the original Nobel Prizes.",
     "Alfred Nobel made his fortune from explosives, and a mistaken obituary calling him a merchant of death may have influenced his decision to fund prizes in his will. The first were awarded in 1901, while the economics prize was added in 1968.",
     ["Alfred Nobel founded the prizes after he was awarded one for inventing dynamite, and the economics prize was among the original categories named in his will in 1895 by his family.",
      "A French newspaper published Nobel's obituary after his death in 1888, and his brother used the family fortune to create prizes, which historians say had nothing to do with explosives.",
      "Historians are certain that the mistaken obituary was the only reason Nobel created the prizes, which were first awarded in 1895 in physics, chemistry and economics, among several other fields."],
     "The obituary story may have influenced the will; economics was added much later."),
    ("Linnaeus's binomial names",
     "Before the eighteenth century, naturalists often identified plants and animals using long descriptive phrases in Latin, sometimes a dozen words long. The Swedish botanist Carl Linnaeus popularised a much simpler approach in the 1750s. He gave each species a two-part name: the first word identifies the group it belongs to, called the genus, and the second identifies the particular species. Humans, for example, are Homo sapiens. Latin was used because it was the shared language of scholars across Europe, and because, as a language no longer changing in everyday speech, its meanings were stable. The system solves a practical problem. The same animal may have many different common names in different languages and regions, while one common name may refer to several different species. A scientific name, by contrast, refers to one species only, allowing researchers anywhere in the world to be sure they are discussing the same organism.",
     "Linnaeus replaced long Latin descriptions with two-part names for each species, showing its genus and species. Latin was a stable, shared scholarly language, and scientific names avoid the confusion caused by varied common names across languages and regions.",
     ["Linnaeus introduced long descriptive phrases to name species, replacing the two-part names used earlier, because common names were more precise than Latin in most languages of Europe at that time.",
      "Scientific names are written in Latin because it was the everyday language of Sweden in the eighteenth century, and each scientific name may refer to several different species in different regions.",
      "The two-part naming system was abandoned in the twentieth century because researchers in different countries preferred local common names that everyone could easily understand without any special scientific training."],
     "Two-part Latin names give each species one unambiguous name."),
    ("Gaps in the periodic table",
     "In 1869, the Russian chemist Dmitri Mendeleev published a table arranging the known chemical elements in order of their atomic weight, grouping together elements with similar properties. Others had noticed patterns before, but Mendeleev took a bold step. Where the pattern suggested an element should exist but none was known, he left a gap, and he predicted the properties the missing elements would have, including their approximate weight and density. He also changed the order of some elements when their properties did not fit, suggesting that their measured weights might be wrong. Within about two decades, several of the predicted elements were discovered, including gallium and germanium, with properties remarkably close to his forecasts. These successful predictions did more than anything else to convince scientists that his table reflected a real underlying order in nature.",
     "Mendeleev arranged the elements by weight and properties, leaving gaps for undiscovered elements and predicting their properties. When elements such as gallium and germanium were found with properties close to his predictions, scientists were convinced his table reflected a real order.",
     ["Mendeleev was the first person to notice any pattern among the elements, and his table was accepted immediately because it included every element that exists today in the natural world.",
      "Mendeleev refused to change the order of any elements, even when their properties did not fit, and his predictions about the missing elements turned out to be inaccurate in most cases.",
      "Gallium and germanium were already known in 1869, and Mendeleev used them to test his table, which scientists around the world accepted only after his death, many decades later, in Russia."],
     "The gaps and predictions, later confirmed, convinced scientists."),
    ("A new definition of the kilogram",
     "For more than a century, the kilogram was defined by a single physical object: a small cylinder of platinum and iridium kept in a secure vault near Paris since 1889. Every other measurement of mass in the world was ultimately traced back to it. This created a problem. When the cylinder was compared with its official copies over the decades, their masses were found to have drifted apart by tiny amounts, a few hundredths of a milligram. Since the cylinder was the kilogram by definition, nobody could say for certain whether it had lost mass or the copies had gained it. In 2019, scientists redefined the kilogram using a fundamental constant of nature, called the Planck constant, which can be measured with extremely precise instruments. The cylinder still exists, but it is no longer the standard, and every basic unit of measurement is now defined by constants rather than by objects.",
     "The kilogram used to be defined by a metal cylinder near Paris, but comparisons showed it and its copies drifting slightly apart. In 2019 it was redefined using a constant of nature, so all basic units now rest on constants rather than objects.",
     ["The kilogram cylinder near Paris was found to have lost exactly one gram, so it was destroyed in 2019 and replaced by a new cylinder made from a more stable metal.",
      "Scientists redefined the kilogram because the Paris vault was no longer secure, and the new definition uses copies of the cylinder kept in many laboratories around the world for comparison.",
      "The kilogram is now the only unit still defined by a physical object, because constants of nature cannot yet be measured precisely enough to replace the cylinder kept near Paris."],
     "Drift between cylinders led to a definition based on a constant."),
    ("Counting between flash and thunder",
     "During a thunderstorm, we see the flash of lightning before we hear the thunder, even though both are produced at the same moment. The reason is the enormous difference in speed between light and sound. Light travels so fast that it reaches us almost instantly, while sound travels through the air at roughly three hundred and forty metres per second. This allows a simple estimate of distance: count the seconds between the flash and the thunder, and divide by three to get the approximate distance in kilometres. Thunder itself is caused by the lightning heating the air around it to extremely high temperatures in a fraction of a second, making it expand violently. Thunder can usually be heard up to about fifteen kilometres away, and safety experts point out that if you can hear it, you are close enough to be struck, so you should move indoors.",
     "We see lightning before hearing thunder because light travels far faster than sound, so counting the seconds and dividing by three estimates the distance in kilometres. Thunder comes from rapidly heated air expanding, and if it can be heard, people should shelter indoors.",
     ["Lightning and thunder are produced at different moments, which is why we see the flash first, and thunder can be heard safely from any distance, so there is no need to shelter.",
      "Sound travels faster than light, so thunder should arrive first, but the flash of lightning is brighter, and we notice it earlier because our eyes react more quickly than our ears.",
      "To estimate the distance of a storm, count the seconds between flash and thunder and multiply by three, and thunder is caused by clouds colliding high in the atmosphere during storms."],
     "The speed difference gives a distance estimate; hearing thunder means danger."),
    ("Astronomical distances",
     "Although a light-year sounds like a measure of time, it is actually a measure of distance: the distance light travels in one year, which is roughly nine and a half trillion kilometres. Astronomers use it because distances in space are so vast that kilometres become impractical. The nearest star to the Sun, Proxima Centauri, is just over four light-years away, meaning its light takes more than four years to reach us. This has an interesting consequence. When we look at distant objects, we see them not as they are now but as they were when the light left them. The Andromeda Galaxy, the most distant object easily visible to the naked eye, appears as it was around two and a half million years ago. Powerful telescopes can observe galaxies whose light set out billions of years ago, allowing astronomers to study the early history of the universe.",
     "A light-year is the distance light travels in a year, used because distances in space are so large. Since light takes time to arrive, we see distant objects as they were in the past, which lets telescopes study the early universe.",
     ["A light-year is a unit of time used by astronomers to measure how long stars live, and the nearest star to the Sun is about four million light-years away from the Earth.",
      "Light from the Andromeda Galaxy reaches us instantly, so we see it exactly as it is today, while only very powerful telescopes can show how nearby stars looked in the past.",
      "Astronomers prefer kilometres to light-years because they are more precise, and looking at distant galaxies tells us about the future of the universe rather than about its early history."],
     "A distance unit, and looking far means looking back in time."),
    ("Chlorophyll's colour",
     "Plants are green because of chlorophyll, the pigment they use to capture energy from sunlight for photosynthesis. Chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light strongly, but it absorbs much less green light, which is reflected or passes through the leaf. That reflected green light is what our eyes detect. At first this seems puzzling. Sunlight contains a great deal of green light, so why would plants waste it? Scientists have proposed several explanations. One recent idea is that absorbing light in two separate colour bands helps plants keep a steady supply of energy when light conditions change rapidly, for example when clouds pass over or leaves move in the wind, protecting them from sudden surges that could cause damage. Leaves also contain other pigments, such as yellow and orange ones, which are usually hidden by the chlorophyll and become visible when it breaks down.",
     "Plants look green because chlorophyll absorbs red and blue light but reflects green. Why they use less green light is still debated; one idea is that absorbing two colour bands keeps energy steady in changing light. Other hidden pigments appear when chlorophyll breaks down.",
     ["Chlorophyll absorbs green light more strongly than any other colour, which is why leaves look green, and scientists agree that this helps plants store extra energy for the winter months.",
      "Plants are green because sunlight contains very little green light, so they have no reason to absorb it, and the yellow pigments in leaves are produced only in the autumn each year.",
      "Leaves contain only one pigment, chlorophyll, and they turn yellow when it absorbs too much red and blue light during long periods of cloudy or windy weather in the summer."],
     "Green is reflected; the reason plants use less of it is debated."),
    ("Pink flamingos",
     "Flamingos are not born pink. Chicks hatch with grey or white feathers, and adults only become pink because of what they eat. Their diet of algae and tiny shrimp-like animals contains pigments called carotenoids, the same family of pigments that makes carrots orange. The birds' bodies break these pigments down and deposit them in their feathers, skin and legs. A flamingo that stops eating enough of these foods will gradually become paler. For this reason, zoos add carotenoids to the diet of captive flamingos to keep their colour. The colour also appears to matter in social life. Brighter birds tend to be healthier and more successful at attracting a partner, and researchers have observed that flamingos spread coloured oils from a gland near their tail onto their feathers, particularly during the breeding season, making themselves look pinker, rather like applying make-up.",
     "Flamingos become pink from carotenoid pigments in their diet, not at birth, and fade without them, so zoos supplement their food. Colour matters socially, as brighter birds attract partners, and flamingos even spread coloured oils on their feathers when breeding.",
     ["Flamingo chicks are born bright pink and gradually become paler as adults, which is why zoos add special oils to their feathers to restore their colour before the breeding season.",
      "Flamingos produce their pink colour themselves, whatever they eat, and the colour has no effect on their health or on their success at finding a partner during the breeding season.",
      "The pink colour comes from carrots, which flamingos eat in large amounts in the wild, and zoos have found that feeding them shrimp makes them turn grey within a few weeks."],
     "Diet provides the colour, which also matters for attracting partners."),
    ("A snake's forked tongue",
     "When a snake flicks its tongue, it is not tasting the air in the usual sense, and it is certainly not preparing to sting. It is collecting chemical particles from the air and the ground. The tongue carries these particles back into the mouth, where they are passed to a special sensory organ in the roof of the mouth, sometimes called the Jacobson's organ, which analyses them. The forked shape of the tongue is particularly useful. Because the two tips sample the air at slightly different points, the snake can compare the strength of a scent on its left and right sides, and turn towards the stronger one. This allows it to follow the trail of prey, or of a potential mate, with considerable accuracy. Many lizards use a similar system, and species that rely heavily on following scent trails tend to have more deeply forked tongues.",
     "Snakes flick their tongues to collect chemical particles, which a special organ in the roof of the mouth analyses. The forked tongue lets them compare scent on each side and follow trails of prey or mates, a system many lizards share.",
     ["Snakes use their tongues mainly to taste food before swallowing it, and the forked shape has no particular purpose, since both tips collect exactly the same information from the air.",
      "A snake flicks its tongue as a warning before it stings, and the particles it collects are analysed in its nose, which is why snakes have such a strong sense of smell.",
      "The Jacobson's organ is found in the snake's forked tongue, and lizards cannot follow scent trails because their tongues are not forked like those of snakes in any way at all."],
     "The tongue gathers scent for an organ in the mouth, and the fork gives direction."),
    ("Following the trail",
     "When a foraging ant finds food, it returns to the nest laying down a chemical trail called a pheromone. Other ants follow the trail to the food and, as they return, add more pheromone, strengthening it. This simple system allows colonies to find efficient routes without any ant understanding the overall picture. In a classic experiment, ants were given two bridges of different lengths leading to the same food. At first they used both, but the ants on the shorter bridge completed their round trips faster, so pheromone built up more quickly on that route, and soon most ants were using it. Because pheromones gradually evaporate, trails to exhausted food sources fade away. Computer scientists have been inspired by this behaviour. Programs based on virtual ants laying virtual trails have been used to find efficient routes in problems such as planning deliveries and directing data through networks.",
     "Ants mark routes to food with pheromone trails that others follow and reinforce. Shorter routes gain stronger trails because trips are faster, while unused trails fade. This behaviour has inspired computer programs that find efficient routes for deliveries and networks.",
     ["A leader ant plans the shortest route to food and instructs the others, and computer scientists have copied this by designing programs that are controlled by a single central computer at all times.",
      "Pheromone trails never fade, so ants continue visiting food sources long after the food is gone, which is why the longer bridge in the experiment was used most often by the ants.",
      "In the bridge experiment, ants avoided both routes because the pheromone was too strong, and computer scientists concluded that ant behaviour has no practical use in planning deliveries or networks."],
     "Reinforced trails find short routes without a leader, inspiring route-finding programs."),
    ("A brainless problem solver",
     "The slime mould Physarum polycephalum is a single-celled organism with no brain or nervous system, yet it can solve problems that seem to require intelligence. It spreads out as a yellow network of tubes, searching for food. When it finds food, the tubes leading to it thicken, while tubes that lead nowhere shrink and disappear. In 2000, Japanese researchers showed that a slime mould placed in a maze with food at two exits would eventually form a single tube along the shortest route. Ten years later, researchers placed pieces of food on a surface in the pattern of the cities around Tokyo. The network the slime mould formed was remarkably similar to the region's actual railway network in its efficiency and resilience. The researchers suggested that studying such simple organisms could help engineers design transport and communication networks that adapt without central planning.",
     "Although it has no brain, a slime mould strengthens tubes leading to food and abandons others, allowing it to find the shortest route through a maze. It even formed a network resembling Tokyo's railway, which may help engineers design adaptable networks.",
     ["Slime moulds have a simple brain that allows them to learn routes through mazes, and researchers used them to plan the original railway network around the city of Tokyo in the 1960s.",
      "When a slime mould finds food, its tubes shrink and disappear, so it cannot find the shortest route, and researchers concluded that it is of no use to engineers designing networks.",
      "The Tokyo experiment showed that slime moulds form networks that are far less efficient than real railways, proving that central planning is always necessary when designing transport and communication systems."],
     "Simple tube growth finds efficient networks, which interests engineers."),
    ("Turritopsis",
     "A small jellyfish known as Turritopsis dohrnii, only a few millimetres across, has earned the nickname the immortal jellyfish. Most jellyfish begin life as a polyp attached to the sea floor, grow into a free-swimming adult, reproduce and die. When this species is injured, stressed or ageing, however, it can do something extraordinary. Instead of dying, the adult sinks, transforms back into a polyp, and begins its life cycle again. Its cells change from one type into another, a process scientists are studying closely. In theory, the cycle could repeat many times. In practice, most individuals in the wild are eaten by predators or killed by disease long before this matters, so the jellyfish is not truly immortal. Researchers hope that understanding how its cells reverse their development could contribute to research on ageing and tissue repair.",
     "The jellyfish Turritopsis dohrnii can turn from an adult back into a polyp when injured or ageing, potentially repeating its life cycle. Predators and disease kill most individuals, but its changing cells interest researchers studying ageing and tissue repair.",
     ["The immortal jellyfish cannot die from any cause, including predators and disease, and scientists have already used it to develop new treatments that reverse ageing in humans in recent years.",
      "This jellyfish begins life as a free-swimming adult and later settles on the sea floor as a polyp, where it lives for thousands of years without changing its form at all.",
      "Turritopsis dohrnii is the largest jellyfish in the ocean, and it becomes immortal only in laboratories, where its cells are artificially changed from one type to another by teams of scientists."],
     "It can reverse its life cycle but is not truly immortal in the wild."),
    ("Eighteen holes",
     "Why does a standard round of golf have eighteen holes? Early golf courses in Scotland had no fixed number: some had five, some seven, some twelve or more, depending on the land available. The Old Course at St Andrews, one of the most influential, originally had twenty-two holes, played out along the coast and back again. In 1764, the golfers there decided that the first four holes were too short and combined them into two, which reduced each half of the course by two holes and left a round of eighteen. Because St Andrews became the leading authority on the rules of golf, other clubs gradually followed its example, and eighteen became the standard. A popular story claims that the number was chosen because a bottle of whisky contained eighteen shots, but historians regard this as a legend with no evidence behind it.",
     "Early Scottish golf courses had varying numbers of holes. St Andrews reduced its twenty-two holes to eighteen in 1764 by combining short ones, and because it became the leading authority on golf's rules, other clubs followed. The whisky story is a legend.",
     ["Golf has eighteen holes because a bottle of whisky contained eighteen shots, a fact confirmed by historians who found records of the decision at St Andrews in 1764 among old club papers.",
      "St Andrews originally had twelve holes and added six more in 1764 to make the game longer, but most other clubs refused to follow its example for many years afterwards.",
      "The number of holes was fixed at eighteen when golf was invented, and every early course in Scotland had exactly the same layout, regardless of the land that was available to it."],
     "St Andrews' 1764 change became the standard; the whisky story is a legend."),
    ("An ancient vending machine",
     "The vending machine may seem like a modern invention, but the earliest known design is nearly two thousand years old. In the first century, the engineer Hero of Alexandria described a device for dispensing holy water in temples. When a worshipper dropped a coin through a slot, it landed on a small pan attached to a lever. The weight of the coin tipped the lever, which opened a valve and released a measured amount of water. When the coin slid off the pan, the lever returned to its original position and the flow stopped. Modern coin-operated machines appeared in the 1880s, when machines selling postcards were installed in London. Today vending machines sell everything from drinks to hot meals, and Japan is famous for having one of the highest numbers of machines per person in the world, partly because of low crime rates, which make it safe to leave them outdoors.",
     "The first known vending machine, described by Hero of Alexandria, used a coin's weight to tip a lever and release holy water. Modern machines began in 1880s London, and Japan now has very many, partly because low crime makes outdoor machines safe.",
     ["Hero of Alexandria's machine used electricity to release holy water, and vending machines then disappeared until the twentieth century, when they were first introduced in Japan to sell drinks and food.",
      "The first vending machines sold postcards in ancient Alexandria, and Japan has very few machines today because high crime rates make it unsafe to leave them outdoors in public places.",
      "In Hero's design, the coin stayed on the pan permanently, so the water flowed until the temple staff turned it off, and modern machines still use exactly the same method today."],
     "An ancient coin-and-lever design; modern machines from the 1880s; many in Japan."),
]

HCS_ITEMS = []
for idx, (title, transcript, correct, wrong, why) in enumerate(HCS_RAW, 1):
    assert len(wrong) == 3, f"HCS {idx}: need three wrong summaries"
    slot = (idx - 1) % 4
    texts = wrong[:slot] + [correct] + wrong[slot:]
    letters = "ABCD"
    HCS_ITEMS.append({
        "id": f"q-pl90-hcs-{idx:02d}",
        "type_id": "pte-highlight-correct-summary",
        "type_name": "Highlight Correct Summary",
        "title": title,
        "prompt": "You will hear a recording. Click on the paragraph that best relates to the recording.",
        "audio_transcript": transcript,
        "prep_time_seconds": 10,
        "time_limit_seconds": 90,
        "options": [{"id": letters[n], "text": t} for n, t in enumerate(texts)],
        "correct_answers": [letters[slot]],
        "blanks": None,
        "model_answer": None,
        "explanation": f"Option ({letters[slot]}) is the correct summary. {why} The other summaries contradict the recording or misstate its details.",
        "points": 1,
    })
